<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Azim ud-Din I of Sulu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">18th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Alimuddin I, Azim ud-Din I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Azim ud-Din I, also called Alimuddin I, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Sulu in the eighteenth century. Seeking Spanish help against his brother Bantilan, who had taken power, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> and was baptised in 1750, an act that divided his court. Suspected by the Spanish of treachery, he was imprisoned in Manila and only returned to Sulu after the British occupation of the city in 1762–1764.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alimuddin, son of Sultan Badar ud-Din I, was schooled first by his father and then in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>, where he became proficient in Arabic and Malay and mastered the Qur'an. He took the throne in 1732 when his father abdicated, but gained full powers and formal recognition only in 1735, when his cousin Nasar ud-Din gave up his claim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Early in his reign he ratified the Treaty of 1737, drawn up in January of that year by Governor-General Fernando Valdes y Tamon and signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> for him by Datu Mohammad Ismael and Datu Ja'far. Its five articles provided for permanent peace, alliance against foreign enemies, free trade, each state's responsibility for breaches of the peace, and the exchange of captives with the return of church images and ornaments.</p><p>In a letter of 12 July 1744 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-spain">Philip V of Spain</a> asked leave for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionaries to preach in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. When the panditas learned that the sultan had agreed, a party formed under Prince Bantilan to expel the missionaries and depose him. Bantilan wounded him with a spear, and the sultan fled with his family to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamboanga-city">Zamboanga</a>. He reached Cavite on 2 January 1749 and was received in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> by Governor-General Juan de Arechederra, and after his baptism took the regnal name Ferdinand I. Whether the conversion was sincere or political was doubted by the Spanish themselves.</p><p>In 1751, before leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, he wrote a letter in Arabic for the Marquis of Ovando to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Maguindanao; inconsistent Spanish translations led to charges of treason and ten years in prison, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> took Manila in 1762. After the deaths of Bantilan and of Bantilan's son Azim ud-Din II he returned to Sulu, where he was welcomed, and later abdicated in favour of his son Israil.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>He is remembered in Sulu as a man of peace and a reformer. He minted coins, organised an army, tried to found a navy and revised the sultanate's legal code and justice system. Called Chief of Pandita for his learning in the Qur'an, he began translations of parts of it and of legal and religious works into the local language and a vocabulary of Islamic terms toward making Arabic the state's official language.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Azim ud-Din I of Sulu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sultan <b>Azim ud-Din I</b>, seeking Spanish help against his brother Bantilan, went to Manila, was baptised in 1750 and was later imprisoned, an episode that divided the court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sulu-sultanate">Sulu Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
